  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA cold front moves through the area today, increasing rain chances \ninto the morning hours with the best chance into the early afternoon \nas the front approaches. Thunderstorms are possible for most of the \nCWA following the front as it makes its way down through South \nTexas. The highest chance for showers and thunderstorms will be \nconfined more towards the north and northeast across the Victoria \ncrossroads(60-70%) with low to medium chances expected across the \nCoastal Plains and inner Brush Country(30-50%). \n\nNot too much of a temperature cooldown as this front is mainly more \nPacific in nature. But nonetheless, we can expect cooler weather and \nlower humidity with temperatures in the upper 70s to low 80s into \nWednesday. Unfortunately, those somewhat cooler temperatures \nincrease Thursday as winds return to the southeast bringing moisture \nback into the area, and highs into the low to mid 80's. Looking at \nhigh PWAT values (1.5-1.9 inches) across the region Thursday, rain \nchances could be slightly elevated for the Coastal Bend and \nneighboring Brush Country counties. Guidance is hinting at a Pacific \nlow making its way into the area next weekend. This could bring in a \nstronger front than the one the CWA will experience today, but \nconfidence ultimately remains low for the time being.",
